1. Getting Input from User
In Ruby, you use gets to take input from the user.
puts "Enter the username"
username = gets.chomp  ## `chomp` removes the newline character
puts "My name is #{username}"

2. Input with Integer or Float Conversion
By default, gets returns a string. Use .to_i or .to_f to convert:

puts "enter the age:"
age = gets.chomp.to_i

puts "Enter your height in meters:"
height = gets.chomp.to_f

3. Multiple Inputs in One Line
puts "Enter two numbers separated by space:"
a,b = gets.chomp.split.map(&:to_i)
sum = a + b

4. Input Validation Example
puts "Enter a number:"
input = gets.chomp
if input.match?(/^\d+$/)
  number = input.to_i
  puts "You entered: #{number}"
else
  puts "Invalid input!"
end


Pro Tip:
Always use .chomp after gets to avoid unexpected newlines in your input string.

 /^\d+$/ — Explanation:
Symbol	Meaning
/.../	Denotes the regex pattern
^	Start of the string
\d	Any digit (0–9)
+	One or more of the previous pattern (\d)
$	End of the string